WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:01.600
- [Sal] We're told that Joe started

00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:02.910
his math homework.

00:00:02.910 --> 00:00:07.400
He finished 23 problems by himself.

00:00:07.400 --> 00:00:12.000
He finished 13 more
problems with help from Sal.

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:14.290
I don't know if they're
talking about me or not.

00:00:14.290 --> 00:00:19.170
And then they say there are
nine math problems left.

00:00:19.170 --> 00:00:20.180
And then they ask us,

00:00:20.180 --> 00:00:25.180
how many math problems did
Joe have when he started?

00:00:25.360 --> 00:00:27.960
So, pause this video and see
if you can work through that

00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:29.673
before we do it together.

00:00:30.610 --> 00:00:33.470
All right, so the way
my brain thinks about it

00:00:33.470 --> 00:00:36.950
is there's some amount
that he started with.

00:00:36.950 --> 00:00:40.510
So, let's put a big bar over here.

00:00:40.510 --> 00:00:44.500
This is some amount that Joe started with.

00:00:44.500 --> 00:00:48.793
So, let me write that, starting amount.

00:00:50.110 --> 00:00:52.690
And then he was able to do many of them,

00:00:52.690 --> 00:00:56.210
so he's finished 23 problems by himself.

00:00:56.210 --> 00:00:58.800
So, let me draw 23 here.

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:03.063
So, he was able to finish 23 by himself.

00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:06.790
Then he finished 13 more with help

00:01:06.790 --> 00:01:09.460
from someone with my name, maybe it's me.

00:01:09.460 --> 00:01:13.720
So, then he did 13 more
with help from Sal.

00:01:13.720 --> 00:01:16.620
So, that's 13 right over there.

00:01:16.620 --> 00:01:20.640
And then the total amount left was nine.

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:23.480
So, the total amount left
has to be able to finish up,

00:01:23.480 --> 00:01:26.150
be just as long as our starting amount.

00:01:26.150 --> 00:01:28.390
So, this is nine right over here.

00:01:28.390 --> 00:01:32.840
So, this is left, thus, 23 was
the ones he did by himself.

00:01:32.840 --> 00:01:37.480
The 13 was with help from me
and then he had nine left.

00:01:37.480 --> 00:01:39.480
So, if you wanna know the starting amount,

00:01:39.480 --> 00:01:42.160
how many math problems did
Joe have when he started,

00:01:42.160 --> 00:01:47.130
you have to add together
23 plus 13 plus nine.

00:01:47.130 --> 00:01:48.540
So, how do we do that?

00:01:48.540 --> 00:01:50.210
Well, let's do it one at a time.

00:01:50.210 --> 00:01:51.950
So, let's just think about
how many he finished,

00:01:51.950 --> 00:01:53.860
and then we can add to
that how many is left,

00:01:53.860 --> 00:01:55.590
and that's the starting amount.

00:01:55.590 --> 00:01:57.240
So, the amount that he finished.

00:01:57.240 --> 00:01:59.940
So, that's this amount right over here,

00:01:59.940 --> 00:02:03.320
the ones he did by himself,
plus the ones he did with Sal.

00:02:03.320 --> 00:02:08.320
That's going to be 23 plus 13.

00:02:08.520 --> 00:02:11.380
Now, 23 plus 13 is the same thing,

00:02:11.380 --> 00:02:13.440
if I just think about
it, I have two tens here,

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:16.063
so that's 20 plus three,

00:02:16.900 --> 00:02:19.000
plus 10 plus three.

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:20.640
That one is in the tens place.

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:22.920
Plus 10 plus three.

00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:27.120
Well, that's the same thing
as if I just look at the,

00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:29.890
if I just look at the
tens right over there.

00:02:29.890 --> 00:02:32.530
20 plus 10,

00:02:32.530 --> 00:02:35.410
plus three plus three.

00:02:35.410 --> 00:02:37.530
20 plus 10 is 30.

00:02:37.530 --> 00:02:39.810
So, this is going to be equal to 30,

00:02:39.810 --> 00:02:42.130
plus three plus three is six.

00:02:42.130 --> 00:02:44.820
So, this is going to be equal to 36.

00:02:44.820 --> 00:02:46.560
So, this whole amount right over here,

00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:50.200
the amount of problems that
Joe either did by himself,

00:02:50.200 --> 00:02:54.960
or with the help of Sal,
that is 36 problems.

00:02:54.960 --> 00:02:56.730
And so, to figure out the starting amount,

00:02:56.730 --> 00:02:58.930
we just have to figure
out the total that he did,

00:02:58.930 --> 00:03:02.040
plus how many are left to
get the starting amount.

00:03:02.040 --> 00:03:05.350
So, what is 36 plus nine?

00:03:05.350 --> 00:03:07.450
Well, the way my brain thinks about it,

00:03:07.450 --> 00:03:08.530
let me just do it right over here.

00:03:08.530 --> 00:03:12.380
36 plus nine is equal to what?

00:03:12.380 --> 00:03:17.350
Well, this is the same thing
as 30 plus six plus nine,

00:03:17.350 --> 00:03:22.280
and six plus nine, you
may already know is 15.

00:03:22.280 --> 00:03:23.580
Another way you could think about it

00:03:23.580 --> 00:03:27.930
is if you start at nine, you
have to add one to get to 10,

00:03:27.930 --> 00:03:32.390
and then you have five left
over from the six to get to 15.

00:03:32.390 --> 00:03:34.490
So, another way to rewrite this,

00:03:34.490 --> 00:03:37.340
30 plus 15 is the same thing

00:03:37.340 --> 00:03:42.340
as 30 plus 10 plus five.

00:03:43.730 --> 00:03:47.720
30 plus 10, this right
over here, that's 40.

00:03:47.720 --> 00:03:52.720
So, it's going to be 40 plus
five which is equal to 45.

00:03:52.900 --> 00:03:56.530
So, Joe had 45 problems when he started.

00:03:56.530 --> 00:03:59.710
He did 23 by himself, then he did 13,

00:03:59.710 --> 00:04:00.840
and he would have nine left.

00:04:00.840 --> 00:04:02.890
And you would see if you start with 45,

00:04:02.890 --> 00:04:06.720
and if you were to subtract
23 and then subtract 13,

00:04:06.720 --> 00:04:09.483
you actually would indeed have nine left.

